---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repo = 'https://github.com/jaym267/Phishing-Preventer-AI-Project';
---
<Base
  title="ScamShield Mail — a quiet guard for an older adult's inbox"
  description="A small Google Apps Script that watches a Gmail inbox for scams, asks Claude whether each new message looks like phishing, and moves the convincing ones aside. It never deletes anything."
>
  <h1>A quiet guard for an older adult's inbox.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    ScamShield Mail watches a Gmail inbox for phishing and scams. Every few minutes it reads new
    mail, asks an AI whether each message looks like a scam, and moves the convincing ones out of
    the way — by <strong>labelling and archiving</strong> them, never deleting. Every decision is
    written to a spreadsheet so you can check its work.
  </p>
  <div class="ctas">
    <a class="cta" href={`${base}install/`}>How to install it</a>
    <a class="cta secondary" href={repo} rel="noopener">Read the source</a>
  </div>
  <p class="note">
    It is built to be boring and reversible. The worst thing it can do to a real email is put it in
    a folder.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    A son, daughter, or grandchild who is worried about the scam email a parent or grandparent
    receives, and who is comfortable following a set of technical steps once. You install it on
    their Google account — with their consent — and you get a plain-language email each week
    saying what it did.
  </p>

  <h2>The six hard rules</h2>
  <p>These are not preferences. They are the design, and the code is arranged so that breaking one takes deliberate effort.</p>
  <ol class="rules">
    <li><strong>It never deletes email.</strong> The strongest thing it can do is label a message, move it out of the inbox, and mark it read. The permission it requests from Google is physically incapable of deleting mail.</li>
    <li><strong>It ships switched off.</strong> Until you deliberately turn enforcement on, it only watches and logs. It will not touch a single message.</li>
    <li><strong>It sends the minimum.</strong> Only the sender, subject, links, and the first part of the text ever leave the account. Never attachments. The log keeps at most 200 characters of any message.</li>
    <li><strong>It fails safe.</strong> Any error means <em>no action</em> on that message and a note in the log. An email is never quarantined because of a bug.</li>
    <li><strong>The key stays secret.</strong> The AI service key lives in Google's own settings store — never in the code, a log, or the spreadsheet.</li>
    <li><strong>The allowlist is absolute.</strong> A sender you trust is never scanned and never quarantined, whatever the classifier might say.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul class="flow">
    <li>Every 10 minutes, it looks for new, unread mail in the inbox.</li>
    <li>It skips anything it has already seen, and anything from a sender on the allowlist.</li>
    <li>It sends a short summary of the message — who sent it, the subject, the links, the first part of the text — to Claude and asks: <em>scam, suspicious, or safe?</em></li>
    <li>It writes one row to the spreadsheet: what it decided, how confident it was, and why.</li>
    <li>
      Only if enforcement is on:
      <div class="branch">
        A clear scam → labelled <code>ScamShield/Quarantine</code>, moved out of the inbox, marked read.<br />
        Something that merely looks odd → labelled <code>ScamShield/Suspicious</code>, left where it is.<br />
        Safe → nothing happens.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>Every Sunday, a friendly email goes to a family member: how many messages it read, which ones it set aside, and how to get one back.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Getting one back</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing is deleted, so nothing is ever lost. Every set-aside message sits in a Gmail label where it
    can be read at any time. Putting one back in the inbox takes one step, and the sender is added
    to the allowlist so it cannot happen again.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <p>
    Google charges nothing. The AI service charges per message — roughly <strong>a few dollars a
    month</strong> for a typical inbox with the default model. The exact figure depends on how much
    mail arrives and which model you choose.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not a spam filter.</strong> Gmail already has one. This looks for the persuasive, personal scams that get past it — gift-card requests, fake bank warnings, someone pretending to be family.</li>
    <li><strong>Not perfect.</strong> It will occasionally set aside a real email, and it will occasionally miss a scam. That is why it starts in watch-only mode and keeps a log you can review.</li>
    <li><strong>Not a product.</strong> It is a personal project you run yourself, on an account you have permission to manage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Honest limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It only sees mail that is still <em>unread</em> when it runs. If the message is opened first, it is not examined.</li>
    <li>It trusts the "From" address for the allowlist, and that address can be forged. Prefer exact addresses over whole domains.</li>
    <li>A scam that arrives as a reply inside a real conversation is only labelled, never archived — hiding the real conversation would be worse.</li>
    <li>Its accuracy has not yet been measured on real data. The spreadsheet computes precision as soon as you start marking rows.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="ctas">
    <a class="cta" href={`${base}install/`}>Install it</a>
  </div>
</Base>
